# Shader variant contract tasks

Source proposal: [ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md](ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md), approved 2026-09-22. The proposal is authoritative; this plan covers its remaining implementation work.

## Goal and constraints

Implement immutable, typed program/stage contracts shared by offline compilation and runtime selection. Register every owned program and fixture, compile each distinct stage configuration once, and preserve working programs until replacements link successfully.

Keep `GpuBindingContract` responsible for resource slots and interface locations, the existing TinyAst/GLSL schema and `ShaderSyntaxTreePreprocessor` responsible for source processing, and `Rendering/Spirv` responsible for binary loading. Shader classes own their declarations; subphases 3.1–3.3 replace the current shared partial files and reflection discovery with attribute-driven generation. No manual program list or general configuration framework is permitted; a focused generator project is allowed if required by Roslyn integration.

Runtime manifests, macro discovery, custom binary reflection, compiler-obedience checks, runtime source hashing, and GLSL fallback are already removed or excluded; do not restore them. Preserve read-only span loading, active-resource queries, normal GL lifecycle diagnostics, and existing incremental build receipts. Ordinary uniforms and per-frame buffer data remain outside variant settings. Do not add output-directory cleanup rules.

All build/test execution must use a validation subagent. Preserve unrelated worktree/index changes. Record source review, unit tests, build, GPU behavior, package, and any live-game evidence separately; existing receipts are a baseline, not proof of these changes.

## Assumptions and decisions to resolve

- Type and API names in the proposal are provisional; settle exact names during phase 2 without changing responsibilities.
- Numeric ranges, explicit supported subsets, and per-program variant budgets must come from the setting/source inventory in phase 1. Do not guess new restrictions that reject currently supported behavior.
- Use the proposed scalar set where supported by the source pipeline; record any unsupported type rather than coercing it to another type.
- The plan requires automated rendering and lifecycle evidence. Live-game observations, if collected, are additional evidence; no new mandatory manual gate or performance target is inferred.
- The older `OpenGL.SPIR-V.BuildAndRuntime.todo` contains historical fallback behavior and is not authoritative for this work.

## Phase 1: Inventory programs, stages, and setting consumers

Outcome: the model and migration have a concrete inventory of supported behavior and ownership.

Inventory and task-to-proposal traceability: [ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md](ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md).

- [x] Inventory owned graphics/compute entry points, program owners, fixture programs, shared vertex stages, entry points, fixed defines, and binding contracts. Include isolated build-validator fixtures; exclude include-only files from the entry-point list.
- [x] Inventory runtime setting writers (`VgeShaderDefines`, `SetDefine`, global graphics configuration, compute callers) against actual shader uses and the current stage/specialization declarations.
- [x] Classify each accepted setting by type, default, aliases, domain/range, owning programs, stage use, and conditional availability. Identify obsolete/no-effect settings and their callers explicitly; do not silently register them as functional settings or silently drop live behavior.
- [x] Record supported program assignments, default assignments, proposed variant budgets, and current default/nondefault paths. Identify source reuse requiring distinct stage identities due to incompatible fixed defines or layouts.
- [x] Record the migration baseline and representative rendering/specialization scenarios, including AO alias behavior, near-field/world-probe toggles, importance-sampling overrides, shared fullscreen stages, and direct-file compute loading.

Gate:

- [x] Every owned entry point and setting writer has an explicit destination or an explained no-effect classification. Domain/budget uncertainties are recorded before enforcement; current behavior and the intentional alias-conflict change are distinguishable.

Completion evidence (2026-09-22): source inventory and second review completed; independent audit-stage-completion review confirmed all five tasks and the gate. The audit independently matched 98 source stages, 60 ordinary program entries and 243 expected binaries, and checked writer destinations, defaults/conditions, alternate pairings, fixed configuration and linked-proposal traceability. Documentation-only changes; no new build, unit, GPU, package or live-game execution was required or claimed.

## Phase 2: Typed models and pure variant resolution

Depends on phase 1. Outcome: deterministic contract construction and selection are testable without assets or a GL context.

- [x] Introduce/refine `GpuShaderContract`, `ShaderStageContract`, `ShaderOption<T>`, `ShaderSettings`, and `ShaderVariantResolver`, each with its own responsibility. Keep bindings separate, publish immutable contracts, and isolate per-instance settings from shared defaults.
- [x] Model explicit stage identity/source/type/entry point/bindings, program stage combinations, accepted option groups, and structural/specialization/fixed uses. Support one option being structural in one stage and specialized in another when types/domains permit it.
- [x] Centralize typed normalization, GLSL spelling, canonical formatting, and specialization argument encoding. Cover declared booleans, finite integer/enum domains, and supported 32-bit scalar specializations; reject nonfinite floats and implicit truncation.
- [x] Resolve aliases from declarations: equivalent alias/canonical values agree, conflicting values fail, omitted values use defaults, and compatibility `null` removes an override. Eliminate dependence on normalization through `double`.
- [x] Implement declarative structural conditions using equality, `All`, `Any`, and `Not`; reject unknown/nonstructural references. Separate constant availability from supported configuration membership.
- [x] Resolve Cartesian products or explicit complete assignment lists. Validate duplicates, missing/unknown values, missing defaults, invalid IDs/types/ranges, reserved/fixed-name conflicts, and variant budgets before GPU allocation or compiler invocation.
- [x] Project one normalized program snapshot onto stages. Produce canonical stage keys, deterministic paths, and typed specialization arguments; exclude aliases, unrelated settings, and numeric values from binary identity. Retain inactive user selections while omitting their effective stage inputs.
- [x] Validate shared stage definitions and output-path uniqueness. Deduplicate by stage identity and effective structural configuration without consumer-order dependence.
- [x] Add focused unit tests for normalization/encoding, alias conflicts, independent settings instances, defaults/domains, conditional constants, explicit subsets, unknown identities/options, path determinism/collisions, fragment-only changes, stage sharing, and budget limits.

Gate:

- [x] Pure tests demonstrate typed, deterministic resolution and useful program/stage/option/value errors. No asset reads, GL calls, binary inspection, or serialized load plans are introduced. Existing production callers remain operational until their migrations below.

Implementation traceability (2026-09-22; implementation, second review, delegated verification and independent completion audit passed): all rows inherit the approved proposal and the phase 1 inventory above. Source paths below are under `VanillaGraphicsExpanded/Rendering/Contracts`; tests are under `VanillaGraphicsExpanded.Tests/Unit/Rendering/Contracts`.

| Task | Proposal section / inventory constraint | Implementation and observed verification |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Immutable models/settings | Main objects and ownership; keep bindings separate | `GpuShaderContract`, `ShaderStageContract`, `ShaderOption`, `ShaderSettings`; binding snapshots; declaration/input-mutation and independent-settings tests |
| Explicit stage uses/groups | Programs and stages; Settings and their stage uses | `ShaderStageKind`, explicit source/entry/bindings, `ShaderOptionGroup`, structural/specialization/fixed declarations; stage combination and cross-stage-use tests |
| Scalar normalization | Settings and their stage uses; inventory representable scalar domains | `ShaderScalar` centrally owns bool/int/uint/float parsing, enum underlying values, source spelling and argument bits; scalar boundary, fractional/nonfinite, enum and culture tests |
| Aliases/defaults | Aliases and defaults; intentional conflict rejection | Declared aliases and immutable `ShaderSettings.With` snapshots; equivalent/conflicting pairs, null reset and incompatible-key tests |
| Structural conditions | Conditional specialization constants | `ShaderCondition.Equal/All/Any/Not`; unknown/nonstructural-reference and complete conditional-availability tests |
| Finite support/budgets | Supported configurations and binary identity; no guessed application limits | `ShaderAssignments` validates full products or explicit complete subsets, defaults and budgets; domain/name/ID/range/subset/budget tests |
| Coherent stage projection | Runtime flow; Aliases and defaults | `ShaderStageSelection` projects one snapshot; inactive selections remain in settings; fragment-only, numeric-only, conditional and cross-stage consistency tests |
| Sharing and paths | Programs and stages; Supported configurations and binary identity | `ShaderVariantResolver` validates shared definitions and portable output-path uniqueness; ordinal canonical keys/default direct paths; sharing/conflict/order/path tests |
| Focused pure coverage | Verification and diagnostics | `ShaderScalarTests`, `ShaderSettingsTests`, `ShaderDeclarationTests`, `ShaderVariantResolverTests`; delegated normal build and existing loader/source-layout tests verify the transition path separately |

Transition boundary: the former mutable `ShaderStageContract` is mechanically renamed to `LegacyShaderStageContract`; the existing stage factory, source emitter, build enumerator and span loader retain their behavior. The new immutable model is asset/GL-independent and does not yet replace the production declarations. Phase 3 replaces the old catalog with registry-derived declarations/adaptation; phases 4–5 migrate its consumers and remove the legacy type. No second production catalog or runtime metadata has been introduced. `GpuBindingContract` still supports mutable declaration construction; published typed stages own defensive, read-only snapshots. Bool/uint specialization representation is covered here; actual source-emission/GPU proof for those scalar types remains in phases 4 and 6 before production use.

Completion evidence (2026-09-22): the independent audit confirmed all nine tasks and the gate, with no remaining findings. A declaration-validation finding was corrected and retested: tessellation evaluation may use default patch levels without a control stage; control without evaluation remains invalid. Final delegated normal shader-enabled build: 98 stages / 243 variants, zero errors, five existing unrelated test-analyzer warnings; the shared contracts also compiled in the net8 ShaderBuildTool. Focused tests: 40 executed / 40 passed / zero skipped (28 new typed-contract cases, five existing loader cases and seven source-layout cases). Receipts: `artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/phase2-build-finalv2.log`, `phase2-focused-finalv2.log` and `phase2-focused-finalv2.trx` in the same directory. These supersede earlier attempts, including the corrected missing namespace import. No GPU execution, package validation or live-game behavior is claimed by this model-only work; those gates remain below.

## Phase 3: Explicit registry and domain declarations

Depends on phase 2. Outcome: a single registry describes every production and fixture program and its supported stage combinations.

- [x] Make `GpuShaderContracts` a cached automatic discovery of shader-owned static contract instances and explicitly reused option groups. Register empty-configuration programs explicitly; reject unknown program/stage identities.
- [x] Migrate the phase 1 inventory, including stage pairings currently embedded in `GpuProgram`, `SpirvInventoryTests.VertexFor`, and related helpers. Register shared stages once and give incompatible definitions distinct identities/paths.
- [x] Declare canonical settings, supported domains, budgets, stage uses, stable specialization IDs, structural conditions, fixed defines, and aliases. Include the bent-normal alias on the short-range AO option.
- [x] Declare how global graphics settings are projected through program group memberships. Distinguish known settings irrelevant to one stage/configuration from unknown explicitly supplied program settings.
- [x] Reconcile no-effect legacy writers with their owners and remove obsolete writes where justified by the inventory. Keep shader-local helper macros/include guards outside the configuration registry.
- [x] If intermediate callers require an adapter, derive it from the registry rather than maintaining a second authoritative catalog. Record its consumers and remove it in phase 5 once migration is verified; retain the public string setter only as the approved compatibility API.
- [x] Add registry coverage tests for all owned entry points/fixtures, valid stage combinations, shared stage agreement, default assignments, budgets, and path uniqueness. Verify current supported selections against the recorded baseline.

Gate:

- [x] Registry coverage is complete, including isolated validator fixtures; no unknown stage silently receives an empty contract. Defaults and supported choices preserve the inventory, with intentional compatibility changes documented.

Implementation traceability (2026-09-22; implementation, second review, delegated verification and independent completion audit passed). All rows inherit the approved proposal and the complete phase 1 inventory; source paths are under `Rendering/Contracts` unless noted.

| Task | Proposal / inventory authority | Implementation and planned evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Thin registry and explicit empty programs | Main objects and ownership; Programs and stages; catalog | `GpuShaderContracts.Registry` references shader-class static properties in adjacent `*.ShaderContract.cs` files; pure source/registry coverage and unknown-identity tests |
| Exact pairs and shared identities | Programs and stages; catalog and three alternate fixture pairs | `ShaderProgramDeclaration` constructs shader-owned contracts and shares equal stage definitions; graphics owners, height-bake passes and test helpers consume declared pairs; shared-instance and pairing tests |
| Typed options, uses, budgets and aliases | Settings and their stage uses; Aliases and defaults; structural/numeric inventory tables | `LumOnShaderOptions`, `LumOnShaderGroups`, `LumOnShaderConstants`; verified 242 program assignments / 243 distinct binaries; conditional/alias/default tests |
| Global projection | Aliases and defaults; writer destinations | `ShaderGlobalSettings.Project` validates global names/aliases, filters through explicit group membership; debug-family broadcasts use those memberships; projection/irrelevance/error tests |
| No-effect writers | Writer destinations and ineffective settings | Removed velocity emissive write, PBR AO/debug-mode setters, trace/temporal exploration writes, obsolete atlas-budget/bind-atlas writes, trace diffuse stride and irrelevant orb writes; actual PIS-mask, trace emissive, resource bindings and engine PBR patches remain |
| Single-source transition adapter | Implementation order; no duplicate authoritative catalog | `LegacyShaderStageContract` derives declarations, normalization and conditions from registered stages; old stage/specialization switches deleted; all-selection adapter parity tests |
| Complete coverage including isolated fixtures | Verification and diagnostics; isolated validator scope | `ShaderRegistryTests`; `BuildValidationShaderPrograms` explicit graphics/compute scopes selected by build-validator CLI; normal build, 60 focused tests, four GPU smoke tests and isolated validator passed |

Transition consumers: `ShaderVariantBuild` and `ShaderVariantSource` still use the adapter to enumerate/emit stages; `SpirvStageLoader` and existing GPU inventory helpers still use its stage API. The adapter ignores other stages' dictionary keys while validating its own typed values; strict direct program-setting validation is provided by `ShaderSettings`, with production setter migration reserved for phase 5. It enumerates complete stage products, matching all currently declared supported products; phase 4 replaces that loop with program-assignment projection. The direct-file compute API temporarily resolves a unique registered compute source by basename, rejecting missing/ambiguous matches; phase 5 replaces that legacy addressing with an explicit program identity. The resource-layout compatibility method retains the height-bake family and ring-buffer basename aliases. These are adapters, not additional stage catalogs.

No-effect world-probe method parameters remain temporarily in shared owner call signatures to avoid unrelated caller churn; their obsolete `SetDefine` writes are removed. Ordinary resource binding and CPU update scheduling are unchanged. The builder's fixture scope is part of its incremental fingerprint. Removing the isolated compute fixture now explicitly changes the selected registry scope as well as deleting its source. Full registry-driven build enumeration/AST emission, runtime snapshot/reload ownership, exhaustive GPU linking and fresh mod package proof remain in phases 4–6.

Verification evidence (2026-09-22): normal shader-enabled build passed with 98 stages / 243 variants, zero errors and five existing unrelated test-analyzer warnings (`artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/phase3-build-final.log`). Focused contract/loader/source-layout tests: 60 executed / 60 passed / zero skipped, including 20 new registry cases (`phase3-focused.log` and `.trx` in that directory). Bounded GPU checks: four executed / four passed / zero skipped, covering graphics failed-reload ownership, compute loading and two ring-buffer direct-file bindings (`phase3-gpu-smoke.log` and `.trx`). Isolated validator passed compiler-byte identity, incremental source/include/default invalidation, output integrity, tool/compiler fingerprints and isolated fixture package checks (`phase3-build-contract-final.log`; detailed receipts in `artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-124359`). The initial build's height-bake helper type errors were corrected before the final build. No full GPU matrix, fresh mod package or live-game verification is claimed.

Completion audit (2026-09-22): the independent reviewer confirmed all seven tasks and the gate with high confidence and no outstanding findings. Remaining adapter/runtime/build migration work is explicitly assigned to later items.

Ownership correction (2026-09-22, explicit user direction): removed the separate domain program catalogs. Runtime shader classes now expose their own static contract instances; debug/height-bake families expose one named instance per owned program. The build tool links only their engine-independent partial declaration files. The enumeration references those exact instances. Runtime graphics owners select their own contracts directly; generic test programs retain identity lookup. Binding construction does not force enumeration, avoiding initialization cycles when a shader's contract is accessed first. Source-only fixtures have dedicated shader declaration classes, including the isolated validator. Follow-up verification passed: the shader-enabled build compiled the same 98 stages / 243 variants; the final incremental build reused its valid receipt; 64 focused tests passed (including four ownership tests), four bounded GPU smoke tests passed, and the isolated build validator passed. No tests were skipped. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/shader-ownership-build.log, shader-ownership-build-final.log, shader-ownership-focused-final.log/.trx, shader-ownership-gpu-smoke.log/.trx, shader-ownership-build-contract.log; detailed isolated receipts: artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-131049. The preceding phase-completion audit covers the initial implementation; this user-directed ownership correction received source review and delegated validation.

Automatic-discovery correction (2026-09-22, explicit user direction): removed the manual owner-reference list. ShaderContractDiscovery reads static get-only properties and readonly fields of exact type GpuShaderContract from the containing assembly once; the resulting resolver remains cached. It ignores compiler-generated backing fields and inherited members, and diagnoses mutable/null/unreadable declarations. Only isolated validator owners opt out with ExcludeFromShaderCatalog. Adding ordinary shader sources and the owner's adjacent *.ShaderContract.cs declaration is sufficient; the build tool already includes those files automatically. No central registration edit, macro discovery, shader-binary reflection or per-frame scan is introduced. Verification passed: normal build compiled 98 stages / 243 variants with zero errors and five existing unrelated warnings; 73 focused tests (including nine discovery cases) and four GPU smoke tests passed with zero skips; isolated build validator passed. Receipts: artifacts/shader-variant-contracts/shader-discovery-build.log, shader-discovery-focused.log/.trx, shader-discovery-gpu-smoke.log/.trx, shader-discovery-build-contract.log; isolated details: artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-133724. These receipts supersede the ownership-only checks for the current implementation.
### Phase 3.1: Implement shader contract and option generation

Depends on the completed phase 3 baseline. Authority: [approved proposal — Shader declaration generator](ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md#shader-declaration-generator) and [migration inventory](ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md). Outcome: a small Roslyn incremental generator can produce shader-owned contracts, option accessors and automatic enumeration from declarations on shader classes. Existing shaders remain operational during this subphase.

- [x] Define attributes for program identity, explicit stage sources/uses, bindings, fixed defines and variant budgets. Define attributed typed option accessors with explicit GLSL names, defaults, domains/ranges, aliases and stable specialization IDs. Use partial properties for generated runtime setters; distinguish any supported declaration fields from writable runtime state.
- [x] Define how attributes reference shared typed options/groups and express conditional availability, including equality, `All`, `Any` and `Not`, without duplicating option definitions or introducing arbitrary executable conditions. Cover multiple programs owned by one class, shared stages, explicit supported assignment sets, and isolated fixture scopes. Settle exact API spelling against the complete inventory before implementation.
- [x] Implement semantic Roslyn generation of immutable static contracts, typed option keys, property implementations and deterministic program enumeration. Generate accessors against a shared settings-update hook; reuse typed normalization and existing reload scheduling rather than embedding a second settings implementation. Test that hook with fixtures now; full coherent loading/reload migration remains in phase 5.
- [x] Integrate generated declarations into the offline build tool and runtime without requiring handwritten `*.ShaderContract.cs` files, runtime reflection, manual registration or the completed mod assembly before shader compilation. Establish an acyclic clean-build order, compatible SDK/language targets, incremental invalidation and independent validator scopes; generated C# is build input, not a runtime manifest.
- [x] Add compiler diagnostics and generator tests for invalid accessor shapes/types/defaults, names/aliases, stage combinations, duplicate IDs/identities, unsupported condition references, shared-option conflicts and budget/domain errors. Compile generated output and verify deterministic results, shared-stage identity, accessor validation and automatic inclusion/removal when a shader declaration changes.
- [x] Run delegated generator/unit tests and a normal shader-enabled build with representative generated graphics, compute, family and isolated-validator fixtures. Prove a fresh build can reach shader compilation without a circular dependency; record any temporary migration bridge and its removal in 3.3.

Gate:

- [x] Generated declarations compile and agree between build/runtime consumers; representative accessors work through the shared hook, diagnostics are actionable, and adding a fixture requires no central registration or handwritten contract partial file. Production migration remains explicitly pending in 3.3.

Implementation traceability for 3.1 (2026-09-22; implementation, second review, delegated verification and independent completion audit passed). Every task inherits the complete approved proposal and migration inventory; its generator section supersedes the older partial-file/reflection authoring target. The exact attribute API/build boundary is recorded in the proposal's `Declaration API and build integration` subsection.

