### Phase 3.3: Migrate all shaders to generated declarations

Depends on 3.2. Authority: [approved proposal — Shader declaration generator](ShaderVariantContracts.proposal.md#shader-declaration-generator) and [migration inventory](ShaderVariantContracts.inventory.md). Outcome: every owned shader uses declarations on its owning class, and the generator is the sole producer of contracts and program enumeration.

- [x] Migrate every production graphics/compute shader, debug and height-bake family, source-only shader owner, packaged GPU fixture, alternate stage pairing and isolated build-validator shader to the attributed model. Preserve explicit empty configurations, bindings, stage identities, entry points and fixed build configuration.
- [x] Migrate effective shader-option accessors and their callers to generated typed properties/shared declarations. Preserve canonical GLSL names, defaults, aliases, numeric IDs, availability conditions expressed through the inline syntax from 3.2, supported products/subsets, budgets and inactive selections. Keep ordinary uniforms, CPU scheduling and engine-owned PBR shader patches outside this migration; do not restore inventoried no-effect options.
- [x] Connect generated accessors to the existing runtime settings/reload path through the shared hook from 3.1. Validate values consistently and preserve existing working-program behavior; record any loader-only bridge still required until phase 5 rather than prematurely claiming its snapshot/coalescing work complete.
- [x] Switch build/runtime enumeration and fixture scopes to generated references. Remove handwritten contract partial declarations, their special source-linking convention, `ShaderContractDiscovery`, reflection-only opt-out metadata and superseded declaration lists/helpers. Retain shared typed models and useful option definitions; leave unrelated build/AST/loader adapters to their assigned phases.
- [x] Verify complete migration against the inventory: 63 packaged program declarations, 98 stages, 242 program assignments and 243 distinct binaries, plus the two isolated validator programs. Check defaults, all finite selections, conditional numeric inputs, alias behavior, deterministic paths and sharing; explain any intentional count change.
