`contract-build-output-coverage.json` verifies the complete resolved path set equals the 243 generated binaries, with exactly 243 compiler inputs and no metadata files. `contract-build-validator.log` and `artifacts/spirv-build-contract/run-20260922-152118/result.txt` record passing source-coverage failures before compilation, fixture counts, compiler-byte identity, incremental invalidation, and fresh isolated package integrity without runtime metadata or build receipts. The existing receipt still fingerprints source/include, compiled contract/fixed/layout/tool and compiler inputs. No new output cleanup rules were introduced.

Earlier attempts exposed an inaccessible TinyAst internal API, a generic keyword matcher that did not match the custom GLSL qualifier kind, and trivia duplication during qualifier removal. The final implementation uses the schema's exact keyword kind and a nonempty syntax-editor range; focused regressions preserve comments exactly once, nested expressions, locals and layout-qualified specialization declarations. The incomplete earlier build was stopped and is not completion evidence. The final normal build and tests supersede those attempts. Full mod packaging, exhaustive GPU combinations and live-game behavior remain reserved for later acceptance; this work verifies the current runtime loader transition with the bounded GPU suite.

Completion evidence (2026-09-22): independent audit-stage-completion review confirmed all eight tasks and the gate with high confidence and no remaining findings or required evidence gaps. Task markers were updated only after that verdict. The runtime-only compatibility adapter and exhaustive acceptance remain assigned below.

## Phase 5: Runtime settings, loading, and reload migration

Depends on phase 4. Outcome: graphics and compute preparation consume one typed settings snapshot and preserve installed programs on failure.

- [ ] Route `GpuProgram` and compute entry points through explicit program contracts and the shared resolver. Migrate asset and direct-file fixture callers without inferring stage identities from filenames or requiring adjacent metadata.
- [ ] Expose typed setting updates and retain `SetDefine` as a contract-backed name/alias adapter. Validate updates before GPU preparation; apply global settings through declared memberships and preserve inactive selections.
- [ ] Resolve all stages from one immutable settings snapshot into a transient load plan: binary selections plus typed specialization arguments. Keep asset I/O in the span-based loader and GL object ownership in existing shader/program abstractions.
- [ ] Coalesce updates through existing scheduling. Compare effective binary/specialization selections so equivalent aliases, unused values, and unchanged settings do not reload; structural/active numeric changes prepare a replacement.
- [ ] Keep requested settings distinct from installed settings. Commit the new program and installed snapshot only after successful linking; release failed candidates and retain the working program/interface on read, specialization, or link failure.
- [ ] Preserve active-resource lookup and normal lifecycle diagnostics without compiler-obedience validation. Keep ordinary uniform/buffer setters outside the variant API.
- [ ] Remove transitional registry adapters, duplicated stage-selection switches, string specialization types, callback conditions, hard-coded alias handling, and obsolete setting discovery remnants after consumer verification. Keep the approved compatibility setter.
- [ ] Add/run settings and GPU lifecycle tests for no-op changes, alias/null handling, fragment-only choices, active numeric changes, disabling/re-enabling preserved values, coherent multistage snapshots, failed replacement ownership, repeated disposal/reload, span slices, and direct-file compute loading.

Gate:

- [ ] Both graphics and compute paths use the same resolver/build declarations. Effective no-op updates avoid preparation, successful reloads install coherent settings, and failed reloads preserve the prior program without leaked candidate resources.

## Phase 6: Registry-driven coverage and developer guidance

Depends on phase 5. Outcome: the complete supported system is verified through the declared registry and documented for future shader additions.

- [ ] Derive stage inventory and program-link cases from the registry; remove filename pairing heuristics and manifest-driven assumptions. Ensure every declared finite stage variant and supported graphics combination is exercised.
- [ ] Run representative numeric specialization defaults/boundaries and rendering regressions selected in phase 1, including world-probe/near-field visibility, importance sampling, uniform/buffer behavior, and graphics/compute reload/disposal. Do not claim exhaustive coverage of continuous/unbounded numeric domains.
- [ ] Run final normal build, unit/source/integration tests, GPU coverage, isolated build-contract verification, and a fresh package check. Record expected versus actual configuration counts, failures/skips and their disposition, and receipts separately by evidence type.
- [ ] Confirm packaging includes every resolved binary, unchanged compiler output, and no runtime manifests or build receipts. Confirm no runtime GLSL fallback, binary parser, source-hash check, or new per-frame validation was introduced.
- [ ] Update developer guidance for adding a program/stage, sharing a stage, declaring typed options/domains/aliases/conditions, changing settings, and diagnosing selection or load failures. Document the intentional alias-conflict behavior and any inventoried obsolete setting removals.
- [ ] Update proposal implementation status and this plan's evidence, clearly distinguishing completed behavior from any unresolved gap. Record live-game observations only if actually performed; do not turn test timings into performance claims.

Gate:

- [ ] All registered configurations have build/link evidence; representative rendering and lifecycle regressions pass, package coverage is verified, and no undocumented transitional path remains.

## Final acceptance

- [ ] Every owned program/stage and application-configurable option has an explicit typed declaration and stage use; unknown settings/identities fail clearly.
- [ ] Build/runtime resolution agrees, stage sharing is deterministic, and supported configurations preserve documented defaults and behavior.
- [ ] The approved no-manifest/no-custom-reflection architecture, shared AST pipeline, span loading, and safe replacement ownership are preserved.
- [ ] Required source, unit, build, GPU, package, migration cleanup, and developer-documentation evidence is recorded; unresolved gaps remain unchecked.

